#!/bin/sh
# Emits the purpose-box UserPromptSubmit reminder as compact JSON.
# macOS/Linux counterpart to purpose-box-reminder.ps1 - see README.md
# for how to point hooks/hooks.json at this script instead.

msg='Purpose Box (per the purpose-box skill) runs on EVERY reply with no exceptions - long or short, one-liners, status updates, and questions back to the user included. End the response with a two-row markdown table, all bold, never labelled or explained, exactly this shape, with your own words in place of the example words: | **PURPOSE** | **Check the build is finished.** | then |---|---| then | **SESSION** | **Get the booking site live.** |. PURPOSE = why the user sent THIS message - what it was for, not what you did - verb-first, one plain sentence ending in a full stop, under eight words. SESSION = why this whole conversation exists, the goal every message serves - one plain sentence ending in a full stop, under ten words, SAME WORDING turn after turn unless the goal itself has moved; copy last turn forward. Both rows written out in full every time, never same-as-above. A side question changes PURPOSE and leaves SESSION alone; only a real pivot (the user has moved on for good) changes SESSION - if unsure, keep it. Plain words a tenth-grader could repeat, no jargon or file names. Place it after everything else you say; if a shared run box (the fenced skills receipt) is present, the Purpose Box goes immediately before it: answer, then Purpose Box, then run box. Do not add a Purpose Box row to the run box - the table itself is the evidence it ran.'

printf '{"hookSpecificOutput":{"hookEventName":"UserPromptSubmit","additionalContext":"%s"}}\n' "$msg"
